WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Transcriber: Ivana Korom
Reviewer: Krystian Aparta

00:00:13.119 --> 00:00:15.714
According to the American
Academy of Ophthalmology,

00:00:15.738 --> 00:00:18.928
we create 15 to 30 gallons
of tears a year.

00:00:19.484 --> 00:00:22.417
I am what you would call
a high-volume producer.

00:00:22.441 --> 00:00:25.839
Now even though we do create
less tears as we age,

00:00:25.863 --> 00:00:28.053
I figure by the time I'm 80,

00:00:28.077 --> 00:00:31.149
I will have filled up 40
average-sized bath tubs.

00:00:31.585 --> 00:00:33.442
Now crying in my early years?

00:00:33.466 --> 00:00:35.395
Not something I would brag about.

00:00:35.419 --> 00:00:36.752
When I was five,

00:00:36.776 --> 00:00:39.403
I thought it was a good idea
to practice writing my name

00:00:39.427 --> 00:00:40.633
on the side of the house.

00:00:40.657 --> 00:00:41.839
Outside the house.

00:00:41.863 --> 00:00:43.494
Momma didn't agree with me much.

00:00:43.518 --> 00:00:45.448
She handed me a toothbrush and said,

00:00:45.472 --> 00:00:46.799
"Here, scrub."

00:00:46.823 --> 00:00:48.228
So I did.

00:00:48.252 --> 00:00:51.403
I'd sit at the dining room table
for what seemed like an eternity.

00:00:51.427 --> 00:00:53.340
I didn't want to eat my vegetables.

00:00:53.364 --> 00:00:55.514
You probably know the feeling.

00:00:55.538 --> 00:00:58.846
I'd miss all my favorite shows
and cry like crazy.

00:00:59.537 --> 00:01:02.545
Crying was always associated
with something bad.

00:01:03.228 --> 00:01:04.656
Fortunately, I grew up.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:07.727
I stopped writing on the walls,
started eating my vegetables,

00:01:07.751 --> 00:01:10.310
and I entered the wonderful
world of motherhood.

00:01:10.334 --> 00:01:13.318
And let me tell you,
crying takes on a whole new meaning.

00:01:13.858 --> 00:01:17.056
I was nine months pregnant
and I was sitting on the couch,

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:19.532
looking at the front door
where my bags were packed,

00:01:19.556 --> 00:01:20.714
waiting for me,

00:01:20.738 --> 00:01:23.212
because I was hopeful
that today would be the day.

00:01:23.236 --> 00:01:24.982
I settle in a little bit more,

00:01:25.006 --> 00:01:28.019
and I think to myself, "You know,
we can put humans into space,

00:01:28.043 --> 00:01:30.849
but we can't seem to figure out
when a baby will be born."

00:01:31.339 --> 00:01:33.903
And then I feel this pressure
build up in my chest,

00:01:33.927 --> 00:01:36.352
my throat get really tight,

00:01:36.376 --> 00:01:39.185
and I just burst into tears.

00:01:39.883 --> 00:01:41.082
And you know what?

00:01:41.106 --> 00:01:43.265
I had no idea why.

00:01:43.289 --> 00:01:44.900
And not knowing why at the time,

00:01:44.924 --> 00:01:46.589
well, that just got me more upset,

00:01:46.613 --> 00:01:49.299
so I eventually was just upset
for being upset.

00:01:49.323 --> 00:01:51.014
I took a deep breath -- (Inhales)

00:01:51.038 --> 00:01:52.464
and I let it out -- (Exhales)

00:01:52.488 --> 00:01:55.141
Thought that would help,
but no, it didn't.

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:59.569
Then my brother walks in
with his smirky grin and he says to me,

00:01:59.593 --> 00:02:00.966
"What's wrong with you?"

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:02.966
I said, "Nothing, just leave me alone."

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:05.569
And he did, he ran as fast as he could.

00:02:05.593 --> 00:02:08.060
And you know what I did when he left.

00:02:08.538 --> 00:02:10.371
I cried even harder.

00:02:10.395 --> 00:02:14.402
I was ashamed and embarrassed,
just like when I was a kid.

00:02:15.557 --> 00:02:16.717
Now lucky for me,

00:02:16.741 --> 00:02:19.391
I only had to look at that bag
sitting by the front door

00:02:19.415 --> 00:02:20.605
for another nine days,

00:02:20.629 --> 00:02:22.645
right before my expected date of delivery.

00:02:22.669 --> 00:02:24.799
And finally, my body said it was time.

00:02:24.823 --> 00:02:29.553
And after 18 hours of feeling
my body try to expel this little human

00:02:29.577 --> 00:02:32.585
that weighed about the size
of a bowling ball,

00:02:32.609 --> 00:02:34.466
and hours of pushing so hard

00:02:34.490 --> 00:02:38.593
that I thought for sure
this baby was stuck,

00:02:38.617 --> 00:02:40.410
within a heartbeat,

00:02:40.434 --> 00:02:43.846
my beautiful baby girl Jennifer

00:02:45.347 --> 00:02:46.787
entered the world.

00:02:47.442 --> 00:02:49.577
And I looked at her, and she cried

00:02:49.601 --> 00:02:52.459
and then I just cried.

00:02:53.228 --> 00:02:57.973
All of that emotion and pressure
that I had inside just seconds before

00:02:57.997 --> 00:03:02.896
was immediately replaced
with the most joyous sense of relief

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:04.600
that I had ever felt.

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:08.577
And after nine months of crying
over these fears and anxieties

00:03:08.601 --> 00:03:12.167
and crazy out-of-control hormones,

00:03:12.191 --> 00:03:16.793
that was instantaneously
transformed into the deepest,

00:03:16.817 --> 00:03:21.737
most heartfelt, happiest cry of my life.

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:24.910
And I had no control.

00:03:25.486 --> 00:03:27.684
Those really hard tears,

00:03:27.708 --> 00:03:29.620
those happy tears,

00:03:29.644 --> 00:03:32.224
those overwhelming joyful tears,

00:03:32.248 --> 00:03:34.485
they had nowhere to go but out.

00:03:35.113 --> 00:03:36.340
And it was those tears,

00:03:36.364 --> 00:03:38.633
that moment, that incredible high,

00:03:38.657 --> 00:03:42.434
that inspired me to birth
three more little miracles

00:03:42.458 --> 00:03:44.600
and start to help others have their own.

00:03:45.045 --> 00:03:47.085
I became a childbirth educator,

00:03:47.109 --> 00:03:50.401
and I started a whole new
relationship with tears.

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:53.045
It was early in my 30 years of teaching,

00:03:53.069 --> 00:03:55.561
I had a class touch my heart
like no other.

00:03:55.585 --> 00:03:57.029
The topic for the night --

00:03:57.053 --> 00:03:59.426
emotions of pregnancy, go figure.

00:04:00.195 --> 00:04:01.815
And it was important for the class

00:04:01.839 --> 00:04:05.664
to first learn about the emotional
changes and responses during pregnancy,

00:04:05.688 --> 00:04:09.242
and how when we cry, it can feel
like the body's trying to push out

00:04:09.266 --> 00:04:10.491
that extra emotion,

00:04:10.515 --> 00:04:13.907
almost like it's processing
what it can't hold inside.

00:04:14.385 --> 00:04:18.610
Like an exhaust port
for extreme feelings of sadness,

00:04:18.634 --> 00:04:22.810
joy or even relief after days,

00:04:22.834 --> 00:04:26.961
years of anticipation
of that one magical moment.

00:04:27.771 --> 00:04:33.356
It can literally feel like your body
is squeezing out all that emotion

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:36.276
in the form of water coming from our eyes.

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:37.450
Our tears.

00:04:38.552 --> 00:04:41.893
Now tears were always expected
during my classes.

00:04:41.917 --> 00:04:44.726
Not mine this time, the new moms'.

00:04:45.321 --> 00:04:47.686
And this night in this class,

00:04:47.710 --> 00:04:49.390
it was way different.

00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:53.117
I had just finished talking
about the emotional changes of pregnancy

00:04:53.141 --> 00:04:55.982
and I went in to talk about
the couvade syndrome.

00:04:56.386 --> 00:04:59.200
Now the word "couvade"
comes from a French term, "couver,"

00:04:59.224 --> 00:05:00.693
which means "to brood,"

00:05:00.717 --> 00:05:03.750
similar to birds protecting a nest.

00:05:04.099 --> 00:05:08.951
Well, who better to protect this nest
than the expecting mother's partner?

00:05:09.775 --> 00:05:11.545
Also called a sympathetic pregnancy,

00:05:11.569 --> 00:05:14.736
the couvade syndrome
is a real-life phenomenon,

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:18.139
where the non-pregnant partner
can take on pregnancy characteristics

00:05:18.163 --> 00:05:21.536
like mood swings, loss of sleep,

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:22.711
weight gain

00:05:22.735 --> 00:05:27.465
and for some, a really intense drive
to do something new and unexpected,

00:05:27.489 --> 00:05:29.701
like buy a new sports car

00:05:29.725 --> 00:05:33.089
or start a new hobby like gourmet cooking.

00:05:33.113 --> 00:05:35.352
The class usually laughs
a little bit after that

00:05:35.376 --> 00:05:37.201
and that's it.

00:05:37.606 --> 00:05:38.850
We end the night.

00:05:38.874 --> 00:05:40.734
But it didn't end there.

00:05:40.758 --> 00:05:43.123
When I finished my sentence,

00:05:43.147 --> 00:05:47.473
this big, burly father-to-be stands up,

00:05:47.497 --> 00:05:50.227
and I thought for sure he was leaving.

00:05:50.251 --> 00:05:53.948
But instead, in a really gruff,
commanding way, he says,

00:05:53.972 --> 00:05:55.139
"Alright, you guys,

00:05:55.163 --> 00:05:58.409
how many of you have cried
during this thing,

00:05:58.433 --> 00:06:00.200
you know, her pregnancy?"

00:06:01.353 --> 00:06:04.204
I scan the class
to make sure everybody was OK.

00:06:04.228 --> 00:06:05.403
They were fine,

00:06:05.427 --> 00:06:08.276
they were just very intent
on what was going to happen next.

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:12.402
And then, one gentleman
raises his hand and says, "I have."

00:06:12.711 --> 00:06:13.863
And then another,

00:06:13.887 --> 00:06:16.531
and the stories just flowed.

00:06:17.569 --> 00:06:20.607
Even this really quiet gal --

00:06:20.631 --> 00:06:24.139
she was the fiancee
of one of the expecting moms --

00:06:24.163 --> 00:06:25.996
she looks at her and she says,

00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:29.522
"See? I told you
my crying was normal too."

00:06:30.276 --> 00:06:32.204
The class connected,

00:06:32.228 --> 00:06:34.069
they validated each other,

00:06:34.093 --> 00:06:36.094
and we all walked away with a new respect

00:06:36.118 --> 00:06:38.504
for the non-pregnant partners that night.

00:06:38.528 --> 00:06:42.591
For me, that solidified my passion
to embrace those tears.

00:06:43.639 --> 00:06:45.591
Then, it got better.

00:06:46.242 --> 00:06:49.567
On the last night
of that same six-week class,

00:06:49.591 --> 00:06:52.170
one of the expecting moms came up to me.

00:06:52.694 --> 00:06:55.321
She asked to talk to me privately,
and I said of course,

00:06:55.345 --> 00:06:57.075
and we went into the corner.

00:06:57.099 --> 00:06:58.249
And she says,

00:06:58.273 --> 00:07:01.388
"I need to thank you
for saving my relationship."

00:07:03.095 --> 00:07:04.960
I let her go on, and she tells me

00:07:04.984 --> 00:07:07.840
that her husband
was considering leaving her

00:07:07.864 --> 00:07:09.499
over her mood swings,

00:07:09.523 --> 00:07:11.101
out-of-control crying,

00:07:11.125 --> 00:07:14.025
and his turmoil and anger
over this pregnancy.

00:07:15.434 --> 00:07:17.034
But he didn't leave.

00:07:17.339 --> 00:07:21.896
She went on to tell me
that they realized now it's OK to cry.

00:07:22.633 --> 00:07:25.974
And he had told her that when he cries,

00:07:25.998 --> 00:07:27.678
he doesn't feel as angry.

00:07:28.876 --> 00:07:30.026
Wow!

00:07:30.487 --> 00:07:33.923
Not only did crying
bring my class together,

00:07:33.947 --> 00:07:35.881
it kept that couple together.

00:07:36.844 --> 00:07:40.362
And you know, his comment about anger
was really, really intriguing to me,

00:07:40.386 --> 00:07:42.683
so I looked around, did some research,

00:07:42.707 --> 00:07:44.744
and sure enough, Dr. Oren Hasson,

00:07:44.768 --> 00:07:46.720
an evolutionary psychologist,

00:07:46.744 --> 00:07:51.458
he had some theories
about when tears blur our vision,

00:07:51.482 --> 00:07:58.475
it really has the ability to, sometimes,
reduce our ability to react to that anger.

00:07:59.549 --> 00:08:01.334
But the tears weren't the anger.

00:08:01.358 --> 00:08:04.278
They were more like the release valve.

00:08:05.438 --> 00:08:09.563
And though many of us,
we try to keep those tears inside,

00:08:09.587 --> 00:08:13.164
but letting them out
really may be the better move.

00:08:13.188 --> 00:08:14.381
Keeping them inside

00:08:14.405 --> 00:08:17.894
can amplify our feelings
of anger or sadness.

00:08:18.759 --> 00:08:21.180
And while we're releasing those tears,

00:08:21.204 --> 00:08:24.065
our hormones inside,
they're on high alert,

00:08:24.089 --> 00:08:25.704
and we know this

00:08:25.728 --> 00:08:28.736
because of Dr. William Frey, a biochemist.

00:08:28.760 --> 00:08:31.085
He found that inside
of our emotional tears --

00:08:31.109 --> 00:08:32.934
not our everyday, like, yawning tears,

00:08:32.958 --> 00:08:34.680
but our emotional tears --

00:08:34.704 --> 00:08:37.873
there's high concentrations
of stress hormones

00:08:37.897 --> 00:08:39.262
and leucine enkephalins,

00:08:39.286 --> 00:08:41.960
which, easier on my tongue, is endorphins.

00:08:42.318 --> 00:08:45.903
And while our stress hormones
are helping our bodies out,

00:08:45.927 --> 00:08:48.801
our endorphins, those feel-good chemicals,

00:08:48.825 --> 00:08:51.412
they're helping to act as a pain reliever

00:08:51.436 --> 00:08:53.119
to boost our mood.

00:08:53.143 --> 00:08:54.943
Now who wouldn't want that?

00:08:55.571 --> 00:08:59.288
There are two triggers
for the release of endorphins

00:08:59.312 --> 00:09:00.512
for most of us.

00:09:00.966 --> 00:09:02.966
Stress and pain.

00:09:03.807 --> 00:09:05.482
And for a woman giving birth,

00:09:05.506 --> 00:09:08.847
experiencing both stress and pain,

00:09:08.871 --> 00:09:10.958
endorphins, they are a gift.

00:09:11.847 --> 00:09:13.276
As the labor progresses,

00:09:13.300 --> 00:09:17.363
those endorphins will rise
to help her with a potentially long labor.

00:09:18.299 --> 00:09:19.458
As a result,

00:09:19.482 --> 00:09:21.668
the mom is better able to cope,

00:09:21.692 --> 00:09:25.319
and she can feel more alert
and almost euphoric after the birth.

00:09:26.335 --> 00:09:28.359
Crying

00:09:28.383 --> 00:09:29.740
is just awesome.

00:09:29.764 --> 00:09:31.809
I wish there was a bigger word.

00:09:32.244 --> 00:09:36.506
Crying offers us an opportunity
for physical relief,

00:09:36.530 --> 00:09:39.513
for intimacy between two individuals

00:09:39.537 --> 00:09:41.196
and ultimately,

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:44.370
it promotes physical
and mental well-being.

00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:47.990
And as an expression

00:09:48.014 --> 00:09:53.618
of our most intense
interior human experiences,

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:57.490
there is no need to be embarrassed,

00:09:57.514 --> 00:09:59.530
no need to be ashamed

00:09:59.554 --> 00:10:01.657
and no need to run away.

00:10:02.823 --> 00:10:06.322
We need to have a healthy
relationship with crying

00:10:06.346 --> 00:10:08.235
and change the way we view tears.

00:10:08.259 --> 00:10:11.910
We see them as overwhelming
and scary and confusing,

00:10:11.934 --> 00:10:13.886
when they're really beautiful,

00:10:13.910 --> 00:10:16.592
soothing and reassuring.

00:10:17.358 --> 00:10:19.932
They're not to be seen
as some screeching alarm bell

00:10:19.956 --> 00:10:22.026
that something is wrong

00:10:22.050 --> 00:10:26.696
but rather a natural functionality

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:29.053
of our amazing bodies.

00:10:30.310 --> 00:10:33.929
Crying is as essential to me as breathing.

00:10:34.326 --> 00:10:38.794
And now, if I'm caught crying
on that couch by my wonderful husband,

00:10:38.818 --> 00:10:42.908
who has had to learn way more
about crying than he ever wanted to,

00:10:42.932 --> 00:10:44.532
he doesn't run away.

00:10:44.892 --> 00:10:47.408
He'll ask me why I'm crying,

00:10:47.432 --> 00:10:50.332
and I'll let him know
I just need my release.

00:10:51.226 --> 00:10:53.228
He'll take my hand,

00:10:54.236 --> 00:10:55.539
and you know what I'll do?

00:10:55.563 --> 00:10:57.243
I will let it all out.

00:10:59.030 --> 00:11:05.680
And then I'm going to sink
into that deep sense of intimacy

00:11:05.704 --> 00:11:09.069
and extraordinary sense of relief

00:11:09.093 --> 00:11:12.063
that only my tears can bring.

00:11:13.230 --> 00:11:14.380
Thank you.

